{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MadronichOpticalDepth",
    "description": "Stage 4 of WRF-Chem's Madronich photolysis (phot_opt = 1): the per-bin layer optical properties SUBROUTINE optics builds, the delta-Eddington scaling and the pseudo-spherical slant-path geometry of SUBROUTINE sphers, and the direct beam that falls out of them (chem/module_phot_mad.F:2547-2649 and :3440-3595, WRF v4.8.0).\n\nEverything here is COMPUTED, not tabulated. The Rayleigh cross section is Frohlich & Shaw's 3.90e-28/lam^(3.916 + 0.074 lam + 0.050/lam); the aerosol optical depth is Elterman's 0.379 (340/lam); the surface albedo is the Demerjian et al. (1980) step function of wavelength. The existing gaschem/fastjx component has none of these: its actinic flux is a bilinear lookup over a fixed standard atmosphere, with no Rayleigh, no aerosol, no cloud and no surface albedo at all.\n\nTWO SUMS THAT ARE NOT RECURRENCES. The Fortran accumulates `tauc` and `tausla` by running addition down the column. Both telescope exactly to a contraction -- tauc(i) is the sum of the layer optical depths above interface i, and tausla(i) the same sum weighted by sphers's slant ratios dsdh(i,j) -- so they are written as contractions and no causal self-reference is needed. Only the two-stream solve downstream genuinely needs one.\n\nTHE O2 TERM IS ABSENT ON PURPOSE. optics forms dtabs = dto2 + dto3 with dto2 = 0.2095 vair ao2. In the shipped configuration ao2 is identically zero over every active bin: srband returns before doing anything because its loop opens with `IF (wl(kl)>205.) RETURN` and starts at kl0 = 30 where wl = 254.8 nm, and independently xs(kl,1) is exactly 0.0 for all 101 active bins. Measured on the reference column, d(1,lev) -- the O2 photolysis rate -- is identically zero, and phot1(n) = d(n+1) drops that row anyway. See FORTRAN_BUGS N22. Writing dto2 here would be writing a term that can only ever be zero.\n\nWHAT THE SPHERICITY BUYS: at the reference column's 48.9 degree zenith the slant column is 1.51875 times the vertical one, where a plane-parallel 1/cos would give 1.52074. Small here, unbounded at large zenith -- which is the regime the Chapman branch of the solar geometry exists for."
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NLEV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 69,
      "description": "Number of radiative-transfer layers, 69 for a clear column."
    },
    "NBIN": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 101,
      "description": "Number of active wavelength bins, kl1-kl0+1 = 101."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "lev": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NLEV"
    },
    "lev_nodes": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": {
        "op": "+",
        "args": [
          "NLEV",
          1
        ]
      }
    },
    "wlbin": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NBIN"
    }
  },
